Mavergreen's own: clang links no libarclite for macOS 10.8 and later.

Why: clang 22 links libarclite for any Objective-C link below macOS 10.11 (ObjCRuntime::hasSubscripting
is false there), and this toolchain ships none, so every -fobjc-arc link for 10.9 failed: "SDK does not
contain 'libarclite' ... try increasing the minimum deployment target". 10.8's runtime has native ARC and
its Foundation implements the subscripting methods arclite back-filled; Apple's toolchains of that era
linked arclite only below 10.8, and an ARC program linked without it runs correctly on 10.9.5 (__weak,
literals, subscripting, @autoreleasepool). Below 10.8 the driver still asks for arclite, as before.

Found by libc++'s own test suite (make_exception_ptr.objc.pass.mm) on 10.9.5; it had also blocked
building family Objective-C as ARC. Not for upstream, which follows Apple's current toolchains.

tests/arc-link-test.sh is the regression test (the driver's link line, with a 10.7 control, on any Mac;
the ARC program's run where x86_64 runs, which on 10.9 is CI's on-mavericks job).

--- a/clang/lib/Driver/ToolChains/Darwin.cpp
+++ b/clang/lib/Driver/ToolChains/Darwin.cpp
@@ -1235,6 +1235,11 @@
   if ((runtime.hasNativeARC() || !isObjCAutoRefCount(Args)) &&
       runtime.hasSubscripting())
     return;
+  // macOS 10.8 has native ARC, and its Foundation the subscripting methods
+  // arclite back-filled: Apple's toolchains of that era linked arclite only
+  // below 10.8, and this toolchain ships none.
+  if (isTargetMacOSBased() && !isMacosxVersionLT(10, 8))
+    return;
 
   SmallString<128> P(getDriver().ClangExecutable);
   llvm::sys::path::remove_filename(P); // 'clang'
